{
  "result": "At dusk, when city lights begin their quiet shimmer, I like to stand on the pedestrian bridge that crosses the river and imagine that every illuminated window is a miniature lighthouse. The traffic below hums a low, continuous chord, a mechanical ocean where rubber-tired vessels navigate lanes instead of currents. In that suspended moment, the border between routine and reverie dissolves; pedestrian steps become drumbeats, neon signs echo constellations, and the river\u2019s black mirror absorbs both anxiety and hope. I think about how civilizations have always gathered near flowing water, persuaded by the promise of motion. Streams carried seeds, rumors, songs, even gods. Today, fiber-optic cables ferry our thoughts beneath seabeds, performing a task once assigned to caravans and gulls. Progress is simply the old impulse wearing new costumes. Yet standing there, I also sense that not everything evolves. The desire to pause, to feel a gust brush the skin and say, \u201cYes, I\u2019m still here,\u201d is probably identical to what a merchant felt one thousand years ago after docking his creaking boat. He inhaled the same damp air, listened to the same lapping cadence, and wondered, perhaps, whether his small life mattered against the sprawling ledger of time. The bridge, the traffic, the luminous windows, all will vanish someday, but that merchant\u2019s question will survive, hitchhiking forward, looking for another pair of eyes at another river\u2019s edge, asking again: \u201cAre you listening to the water, and if so, what story does it tell you?\u201d Perhaps it whispers, \u201cBegin again.\u201d",
